# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Two defects, both measured on a CLEAN tree at master, both invisible in CI.
#
# ★ (1) A GATE WHOSE VERDICT WAS A PROPERTY OF THE CHECKOUT HOST.
#
# The repo had no .gitattributes, so line endings were decided by each developer's
# `core.autocrlf` (git's Windows default is `true`). Every blob in the index is LF;
# a Windows working tree therefore held CRLF. `tests/engagement-report-mirror.test.ts`
# extracted the embedded markdown with a bare LF anchored right after the script tag,
# and the byte there was CR. That gate was green on the Linux runner and
# DETERMINISTICALLY RED on the maintainer's own clean checkout — which is the shape
# that teaches people to stop trusting a local run, and it generalises to any future
# assertion that reads bytes.
#
# `eol=lf` makes the working tree match the index and the runner on every platform.
#
# ★ (2) A SOURCE FILE THAT LANDED WITH NO REVIEWABLE DIFF.
#
# `tools/docs-claim-lint.mjs` — a whole new gate — contained a raw NUL byte inside a
# template literal used as a Set key. git classifies a file with a NUL as binary, so it
# landed as `Bin 0 -> 10308 bytes`: zero reviewable content on GitHub and locally,
# `grep` reporting only "Binary file … matches", and every future edit to it —
# including additions to its own KNOWN_BROKEN pin list — rendering the same way. Four
# older files had the same shape. All five now spell that character as the
# six-character JavaScript escape (backslash, u, 0, 0, 0, 0), which denotes exactly the
# same string, so behaviour is unchanged and the files are text.
#
# `text` and `diff` on every source extension is the guard against the next one: `diff`
# forces git to produce a textual diff even where a control byte appears, so a stray
# byte shows up in review instead of silently making the file undiffable.
#
# NOTE: setting these attributes does NOT rewrite an existing working tree. After
# changing this file, re-check out (`git checkout-index -a -f`) or the tree keeps the
# endings it already has. tests/line-endings-are-normalised.test.ts is what notices.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Source: force text classification AND a textual diff.
*.ts     text eol=lf diff
*.tsx    text eol=lf diff
*.mts    text eol=lf diff
*.cts    text eol=lf diff
*.js     text eol=lf diff
*.jsx    text eol=lf diff
*.mjs    text eol=lf diff
*.cjs    text eol=lf diff
*.json   text eol=lf diff
*.jsonld text eol=lf diff
*.md     text eol=lf diff
*.html   text eol=lf diff
*.css    text eol=lf diff
*.ttl    text eol=lf diff
*.shacl  text eol=lf diff
*.nq     text eol=lf diff
*.yml    text eol=lf diff
*.yaml   text eol=lf diff
*.sh     text eol=lf diff
*.sql    text eol=lf diff
*.xml    text eol=lf diff
*.svg    text eol=lf diff
*.txt    text eol=lf diff
Dockerfile*    text eol=lf diff
*.dockerfile   text eol=lf diff

# ★ CRLF ON PURPOSE. cmd.exe reads batch files line by line and a `goto`/label in an
# LF-only .bat can be mis-seeked; these are the only files in the tree whose runtime
# is a Windows shell. Excluded from the normalisation gate by the same extension list.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff binary
